1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:03,834
Three questions, as old as the world.

2
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:08,207
Who makes it? Who keeps it? And who, in the end, lets it go?

3
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:18,951
The first answer is Brahma, the creator, born on a lotus, with four faces that look out in four directions.

4
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:25,665
From him came the Vedas, the oldest sacred knowledge, and from his thought, the world itself.

5
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:32,884
A single day of Brahma lasts 4,320,000,000 years.

6
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:46,398
The second answer is Vishnu, the preserver, at rest on the coils of the serpent Shesha, on the ocean between the worlds.

7
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:52,504
Whenever the world falls out of balance, he returns, in a new form for each age.

8
00:00:53,150 --> 00:01:00,150
A fish. A great tortoise. A wild boar. Half man, half lion. A dwarf.

9
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:08,900
A warrior. A prince. A cowherd. A teacher. And a horseman, still to come.

10
00:01:10,850 --> 00:01:19,343
The third answer is Shiva, the destroyer, the ascetic of Mount Kailash, who caught the falling river Ganga in his hair.

11
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:29,860
His dance keeps the rhythm of the cosmos: in one hand, the drum of creation; in another, the fire that ends it.

12
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:38,135
Four ages turn, each shorter and darker than the last. At the end of the last, he dances the world away.

13
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:50,050
Creator, preserver, destroyer. Not three gods apart, but three faces of one reality.

14
00:01:51,050 --> 00:01:56,699
The Trimurti. And when the last page is turned, the first is painted again.
